INT. SMALL CLAIMS COURT - LIVESTOCK BARN, COUNTY FAIRGROUNDS - DAY

A long barn with sawdust floors and a raised dais built from hay pallets. A flag, a gavel, a laminated seal. Pens along the walls hold sleepy goats. The air smells of hay and floor wax.

JUDGE HORTENSE BEAUMONT-LEE, 60s, in a robe over denim, presides from behind a folding table. To her left sits the CLERK, WINNIFRED TOLLIVER, 50s, with a stack of forms and a cowbell in lieu of a bailiff's staff. At the plaintiff's table: ARCHIBALD "ARCHIE" SPENCE, 40s, a farmer in clean overalls. At the defendant's: DOROTHEA WEBB, 40s, a beekeeper in a veil pushed back. Between them stands a crate that buzzes faintly.

JUDGE HORTENSE
This court is in session. Mr. Spence, you allege that Ms. Webb's bees ate your clover.

ARCHIE
Not ate, Your Honor. Used.

JUDGE HORTENSE
Used.

ARCHIE
Eight acres. Every blossom. They didn't pay.

DOROTHEA
(muttering)
Bees don't carry cash.

JUDGE HORTENSE
Ms. Webb, I heard that.

DOROTHEA
It was meant for the record.

WINNIFRED
(to the room)
Please silence your phones and your livestock.

In the front row sits TRUMAN OKONKWO, 70s, a fairground vendor in a paper hat, eating a corn dog.

TRUMAN
(to the gallery)
This is better than the pie contest.

A goat bleats. Everyone looks at it. The Judge sighs and taps her gavel.

JUDGE HORTENSE
Mr. Spence. What do you want?

ARCHIE
Honey.

JUDGE HORTENSE
Ms. Webb. What do you say?

DOROTHEA
I say he's welcome to a jar.

JUDGE HORTENSE
Done. Next case.

The crate buzzes louder. Winnifred rings the cowbell.
